[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds every report about dates and calls it one issue with one fix.
Its own table already says otherwise. Read report by report, the reports show separate problems
with separate fixes, and each now has a ticket of its own.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix closes the parser ticket only. The form hint and the one-day moves stay broken
until each gets its own fix.
</Callout>

## Day and month swap: the parser

The shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the day first, so
7 April comes back as 4 July. It reaches people through the app, by email and through the
booking-v2 form.

<ReportQuote id="R-41200" />

## The booking-v3 form hint

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type the month first, as the hint
tells them, and the form reads the day first. It hits only bookings made through that form, on
tenants that run booking-v3. The parser fix does not reach it: the hint has to say what the form
reads.

<ReportQuote id="R-41230" />

## Dates that move by one day

Not a swap: the date lands one day off, and one meeting can land on two days for people in two
places. It looks like a time zone shift; that is not confirmed. The desk already lists these
reports in its notes, but the kb files them under the parser by tenant and only footnotes them.

<ReportQuote id="R-41344" />

## How the reports were split

- One report per id. The export repeats some rows, so rows are not reports.
- The desk's one-day list first, then the cause by tenant and way in (`causes.ts`).
- Never the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser: every en-US browser was tagged `hint`.
- Every report lands in exactly one row. The table's last line checks the rows against #4471.

